UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 26, Lesson 1: Simple machines: levers', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nLabel effort, load and fulcrum on a classroom see-saw or ruler-and-block lever.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCorrect three-part labels on a real or drawn lever.\n\nWATCH FOR\nPushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nBuild a ruler lever. Move the fulcrum and record how effort changes for the same load. Draw two labelled diagrams.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-DDT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Build a ruler lever. Move the fulcrum and record how effort changes for the same load. Draw two labelled diagrams. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever. Reasoning: I used lever by attending to fulcrum rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. Correction: Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever. Why: the correction restores accurate use of lever.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses lever. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about lever, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link. / Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever. This matches the lesson skill for lever.","options":["Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever.","Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever."],"question":"Label effort, load and fulcrum on a classroom see-saw or ruler-and-block lever. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels.","Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever.","Careful use of fulcrum with clear evidence.","Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named fulcrum, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the fulcrum used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded."],"question":"A learner is practising lever. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence.","options":["Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever.","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone.","Decide from which object looks most exciting.","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation."],"question":"New examples: steam from a kettle, a germinating bean, a plastic dinosaur, a granite pebble. Which habit makes a scientific sort or claim trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim.","options":["A longer story with no observation.","A label copied from the title with no evidence.","Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever.","Named observation plus how it supports the claim."],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever. applies the science idea with evidence.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever.","Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result."],"question":"Using this idea — A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum… — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable and control the rest.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster.","Stop early when you like the first result.","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer.","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same."],"question":"Which investigation habit keeps a comparison fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs a new object or context with evidence.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Test or describe a new object or context and cite fresh evidence.","Repeat the worked example objects unchanged.","Invent a dramatic result with no observation."],"question":"Which next step best shows transfer of lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses lever. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever. Reasoning: I used lever by attending to fulcrum rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason.","question":"Name one science misconception about lever, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. Correction: Correct three-part labels on a real or drawn lever. Why: the correction restores accurate use of lever.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w26-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise lever.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Engineering and Design","success_criteria":["I can use lever with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify fulcrum and explain why it matters.","I can practise load independently on a new prompt.","I can notice effort needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSee-saw: fulcrum in middle. Bottle opener: fulcrum near the cap. Longer effort arm can make lifting easier for the same load (simplified model).\n\nTRY FIRST\nLabel effort, load and fulcrum on a classroom see-saw or ruler-and-block lever.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCorrect three-part labels on a real or drawn lever.","explain":"A lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Levers.","caption":"Move fulcrum, change effort.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Lever</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bar + fulcrum</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Effort / load</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">labelled</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: See-saw: fulcrum in middle. Bottle opener: fulcrum near the cap. Longer effort arm can make lifting easier for the same  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: See-saw: fulcrum in middle. Bottle opener: fulcrum near the cap. Longer effort arm can make lifting easier for the same  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSee-saw: fulcrum in middle. Bottle opener: fulcrum near the cap. Longer effort arm can make lifting easier for the same load (simplified model). Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Labelled lever trials.","caption":"Effort notes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Ruler build</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">two diagrams</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no fulcrum named</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. Here is a more productive model: See-saw: fulcrum in middle. Bottle opener: fulcrum near the cap. Longer effort arm can make lifting easier for the same load (simplified model).\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCorrect three-part labels on a real or drawn lever.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Pushing without a fulcrum idea is just moving an object, not explaining a lever. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about lever?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA lever is a rigid bar that turns on a fulcrum to move a load with effort; changing fulcrum position changes how hard the effort feels. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 26, Lesson 1: Simple machines: levers","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Mechanisms and systems","week":26,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nSee-saw: fulcrum in middle. Bottle opener: fulcrum near the cap. Longer effort arm can make lifting easier for the same load (simplified model).\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Levers.","caption":"Move fulcrum, change effort.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Lever</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\